1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,325
There are two problems with applying this directly.

2
00:00:03,325 --> 00:00:06,875
The first is that the background is not quite random.

3
00:00:06,875 --> 00:00:15,250
We insist the shuffled sequences keep their lengths, and that constraint can make the distribution non-Gaussian, which throws the estimate off.

4
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:18,450
The second is the size of the database.

5
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:26,525
With a million sequences you have a million chances to find a high score by accident — and the databases are far larger than that now.

6
00:00:26,525 --> 00:00:33,750
The remedy is to adjust the significance level by the number of attempts, which is exactly what the BLAST statistics do.

7
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:38,950
One more tool: relative entropy, borrowed from information theory.

8
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:45,750
For a given substitution matrix you can calculate the relative entropy of aligning a single pair of residues.

9
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:52,000
It is measured in bits, and the alignment's total is that figure times the number of aligned residues.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,125
The useful rule of thumb is thirty bits.

11
00:00:55,125 --> 00:00:59,750
An alignment carrying at least thirty bits is statistically significant.

12
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:10,050
The numbers at the bottom work this through for PAM10 and PAM250, and they lead to a conclusion worth keeping: to detect distant homologues, you need longer alignments.

13
00:01:10,650 --> 00:01:16,975
This summarises the perspectives and pitfalls sections of chapter three, which are worth reading in full.

14
00:01:17,575 --> 00:01:21,800
Now multiple sequence alignment, and first, what it is for.

15
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:28,900
Conserved columns across an alignment mark positions that matter — enzyme active sites, binding domains.

16
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:33,675
That is how Pfam, SMART and InterPro recognise protein families.

17
00:01:33,675 --> 00:01:38,175
The catalytic triad of the serine proteases shows up this way.

18
00:01:38,175 --> 00:01:41,500
Alignments also drive structure prediction.

19
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:50,525
Where two positions vary together across a family, they are probably in contact, and that is the signal AlphaFold and its predecessors exploit.

20
00:01:50,525 --> 00:01:55,075
And they annotate unknown proteins by relating them to known ones.

21
00:01:55,675 --> 00:01:59,650
There is also a structure-based approach, which this lecture skips.

22
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:04,800
It needs ideas from the protein structure lecture; come back to it afterwards.

23
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:07,850
The exact approach first.

24
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:13,900
It is Needleman-Wunsch extended from two sequences to N, and it has the same three steps.

25
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:18,875
Build the matrix — now N-dimensional, with a zeroth plane for every pair.

26
00:02:18,875 --> 00:02:24,950
Easy to write down for a computer, impossible to draw, so the slide shows three sequences.

27
00:02:24,950 --> 00:02:33,250
Fill it by the same recurrence, except that now a gap can go into any of the N sequences, so there are N gap terms rather than two.

28
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:35,150
Then trace back.

29
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:39,950
It guarantees the best alignment, because it considers all of them.

30
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:45,875
And it is only usable for about five sequences, because the cost grows with the dimension.

31
00:02:46,475 --> 00:02:49,425
So in practice, progressive alignment.

32
00:02:49,425 --> 00:02:55,950
The idea is to build the alignment up, starting with the sequences that are most alike and working outwards.

33
00:02:55,950 --> 00:02:57,575
Three steps.

34
00:02:57,575 --> 00:03:03,750
Align every pair of sequences, and use the results to build a matrix of distances between them.

35
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:06,850
Turn that into a guide tree, by clustering.

36
00:03:06,850 --> 00:03:13,900
Then align along the tree: the closest pair first, merged into a group, then the next closest, until everything is in.

37
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:22,675
It is fast and it follows the evolutionary relationships, which is why ClustalW, Clustal Omega and T-Coffee all work this way.

38
00:03:22,675 --> 00:03:25,000
The weakness is in the name.

39
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,150
It is progressive, so it never goes back.

40
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:35,275
An error in the guide tree propagates into everything built on it, and a gap introduced early is never reconsidered.

41
00:03:35,875 --> 00:03:38,125
Let us do one properly.

42
00:03:38,125 --> 00:03:41,525
Ten sequences, lengths from nine to twelve.

43
00:03:41,525 --> 00:03:48,650
First, all forty-five pairwise alignments — forty-five rather than ninety, because identity is symmetric.

44
00:03:48,650 --> 00:03:52,275
Needleman-Wunsch gives the identity matrix shown.

45
00:03:52,275 --> 00:03:56,650
In the balloon on the left is the alignment of sequences three and ten.

46
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:02,825
Notice that identity is calculated against the length of the alignment, not of either sequence.

47
00:04:02,825 --> 00:04:07,375
That matters in the next example, where both sequences have gaps.

48
00:04:07,375 --> 00:04:16,200
Normalising by the upper sequence would make the answer depend on which sequence you put on top — six out of nine one way, six out of ten the other.

49
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:19,275
Normalising by the alignment does not.

50
00:04:19,875 --> 00:04:23,825
Turn identity into distance by this simple relation.

51
00:04:24,425 --> 00:04:26,700
Then build the guide tree.

52
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:33,450
There are many ways; this is the simplest and the most common, hierarchical clustering by average linkage.

53
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:36,875
Start with every sequence as its own cluster.

54
00:04:36,875 --> 00:04:39,075
Find the two closest.

55
00:04:39,075 --> 00:04:40,625
Merge them.

56
00:04:40,625 --> 00:04:46,700
Recompute the distances from the new cluster to everything else, averaging over the members.

57
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:49,650
Repeat until one cluster is left.

58
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:55,050
Drawn as a dendrogram, which gives the order the alignment will be built in.

59
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:59,025
The sequence numbers along the bottom can be in any order.

60
00:04:59,625 --> 00:05:04,925
First merge: sequences three and ten, distance nine, the smallest in the matrix.

61
00:05:05,525 --> 00:05:08,375
Then one and nine, and two and five.

62
00:05:08,375 --> 00:05:14,975
Sequences six and nine are also at distance ten, so six could have gone first and one added after.

63
00:05:14,975 --> 00:05:16,975
Either order works.

64
00:05:17,575 --> 00:05:21,250
Now sequence six joins the cluster of one and nine.

65
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:27,650
The average-linkage denominator is two, because one cluster has a single member and the other has two.

66
00:05:28,250 --> 00:05:33,150
Then the next smallest distance, twenty-two, between sequences four and eight.

67
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:37,975
Now two clusters merge — three and ten with six, one and nine.

68
00:05:37,975 --> 00:05:44,625
Averaging over all six pairs gives twenty-two point five, just above the four-eight distance of twenty-two.

69
00:05:45,225 --> 00:05:50,075
Sequence seven joins two and five, because that is where it is closest.

70
00:05:50,675 --> 00:05:54,675
Then the cluster of four and eight joins seven, two and five.

71
00:05:54,675 --> 00:05:57,375
That takes six distance values.

72
00:05:57,975 --> 00:06:03,125
And the final merge, at thirty-seven point eight, averaged over twenty values.

73
00:06:03,125 --> 00:06:06,850
The distances themselves mean nothing in isolation.

74
00:06:06,850 --> 00:06:13,125
What matters is the order they put the sequences in, because that is the order the alignment is built.

75
00:06:13,725 --> 00:06:24,600
So: align the most closely related sequences first, then merge the groups following the tree, carrying the gaps from the pairwise alignments of the shorter sequences into the longer ones.

76
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:29,700
The second level of the dendrogram is triplets, and there are two of them.

77
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:33,900
In the first, sequence six joins the alignment of one and nine.

78
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:37,475
No gaps are needed — all three are the same length.

79
00:06:37,475 --> 00:06:42,725
In the second we do need gaps, because sequence seven is shorter than sequence two.

80
00:06:42,725 --> 00:06:47,125
We take the gap positions from the pairwise alignment of two and seven.

81
00:06:47,725 --> 00:06:50,800
The third level joins a triplet to a pair.

82
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:59,050
Here the pair of three and ten joins the triplet six, one and nine, with three and ten kept at the bottom because they are closer to each other.

83
00:06:59,050 --> 00:07:07,950
Gaps go into six, one and nine, taken from their alignments against sequence three — or against ten, which would do equally well, since neither has gaps.

84
00:07:08,550 --> 00:07:11,500
The second triplet-plus-pair on that level.

85
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:18,800
Here the gaps can only come from the alignments against sequence two, because that is the only one without gaps of its own.

86
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:21,550
And the final alignment.

87
00:07:21,550 --> 00:07:28,475
Again the extra gaps came from the alignments against sequence three, and sequence ten would have served just as well.

88
00:07:29,075 --> 00:07:31,300
Which points at the weakness.

89
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:36,500
The gaps could have come from either sequence, so the result is not quite determined.

90
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:43,550
The remedy is to refine iteratively: score the alignment, find the regions dragging the score down, and realign them.

91
00:07:43,550 --> 00:07:49,075
MUSCLE and MAFFT both do this, and PRANK does an evolution-aware version.

92
00:07:49,075 --> 00:07:59,075
It is worth the extra time when the alignment feeds something that depends on getting it right — a phylogeny, a structural study, a comparative genomics analysis.

93
00:07:59,675 --> 00:08:02,750
The other improvement is consistency.

94
00:08:02,750 --> 00:08:04,900
The idea is simple.

95
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:11,675
If A aligns to B, and B aligns to C, then the alignment of A to C ought to agree with what those two imply.

96
00:08:11,675 --> 00:08:19,200
So instead of scoring each pair on its own, score it against what the indirect alignments through every other sequence suggest.

97
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:25,925
That information is then used while merging along the guide tree, which stops local misalignments taking hold.

98
00:08:25,925 --> 00:08:31,325
T-Coffee is built around this, and MAFFT uses it in some of its modes.

99
00:08:31,925 --> 00:08:35,300
And finally, the three approaches side by side.

100
00:08:35,300 --> 00:08:44,200
Progressive alignment is fast and follows the evolutionary relationships, but errors in the guide tree carry through and nothing is ever corrected.

101
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:54,375
Iterative alignment starts from a progressive one and refines it, so it does correct its mistakes — at more computational cost, and still limited by where it started.

102
00:08:54,375 --> 00:09:03,150
Consistency-based alignment uses information from all the pairwise alignments at once, which makes it the most accurate of the three and the slowest.

103
00:09:03,150 --> 00:09:05,550
Each suits a different job.

104
00:09:05,550 --> 00:09:09,150
For a large family where you need speed, progressive.

105
00:09:09,150 --> 00:09:13,025
Where accuracy matters more than time, one of the other two.

106
00:09:13,625 --> 00:09:17,100
To close, the three things this lecture rests on.

107
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:32,250
Pairwise alignment is a search for the best correspondence between two sequences, and dynamic programming finds it exactly rather than by trying everything — Needleman-Wunsch for the whole length of both sequences, Smith-Waterman for the best matching region inside them.

108
00:09:32,250 --> 00:09:35,700
A scoring matrix is where the biology enters.

109
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:45,025
It says how much a substitution costs, and a matrix built from closely related proteins asks a different question from one built from distant ones.

110
00:09:45,025 --> 00:09:54,025
And a multiple alignment is built up rather than solved: the most similar sequences are aligned first, then progressively more distant ones are added.

111
00:09:54,025 --> 00:10:01,850
The order is decided by the distances between them, which is why the distance matrix comes before the alignment and not after.

